      "title": "Iran’s Executive Branch: President, Ministers and Administration | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore how Iran’s executive operates through the president and ministers, with parliamentary accountability and constitutional limits."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "executive-remit",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The executive’s remit",
        "content": "Article 60 assigns executive functions to the president and ministers, excluding matters constitutionally reserved to the Leadership. Article 113 places the president below the Leadership in the official hierarchy. These provisions define an executive with significant administrative responsibilities but a bounded jurisdiction. [s1]\n\nThe president’s responsibilities include national planning, the budget, and state employment affairs, although their administration can be delegated. This makes the presidency important to the organization of government even where it does not control the state’s ultimate strategic direction. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cabinet-and-administration",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From appointments to administrative action",
        "content": "Ministers are appointed by the president and presented to parliament for approval. They answer to both institutions for their responsibilities; cabinet decisions also carry collective responsibility. The executive therefore depends on relationships across institutional boundaries when assembling and maintaining its ministerial team. [s2]\n\nAdministration also requires rules below the level of parliamentary legislation. GlobaLex’s legal-system overview explains that individual ministers and the Council of Ministers can issue administrative regulations to implement laws. This distinction separates a statute adopted through the legislative process from the executive measures used to put it into effect. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "executive-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Selected executive relationships",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  P[\"President\"] -->|\"Appoints\"| M[\"Ministers\"]\n  A[\"Parliament\"] -->|\"Votes on confidence\"| M\n  M -->|\"Answer for ministerial responsibilities\"| P\n  M -->|\"Answer for ministerial responsibilities\"| A\n  C[\"Council of Ministers\"] -->|\"Issues implementing regulations\"| R[\"Administrative rules\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows distinguish appointment, parliamentary confidence, accountability, and regulation-making. This is a selection of relationships, not a complete map of executive authority.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "external-representation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Representing the state",
        "content": "Executive responsibilities extend to international representation. The president signs international agreements after the required parliamentary approval, while ambassadors are appointed on the foreign minister’s recommendation with presidential approval. These procedures illustrate how diplomacy combines executive action with other institutions’ roles. [s1]\n\nGlobaLex also identifies promulgating legislation and arranging its implementation among presidential duties. The president’s signature at this stage should therefore be understood within the legally prescribed implementation process. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "limits-in-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Administrative responsibility and political constraints",
        "content": "The Council on Foreign Relations describes presidential policymaking as operating within parameters set by the Supreme Leader. Freedom House’s assessment similarly places ultimate power with the Leader and institutions under that office’s control. These independent accounts distinguish the executive’s visible administrative role from comprehensive control over the state. [s3][s4]\n\nThe BTI 2026 report goes further, arguing that institutional constraints and a limited independent political base restricted presidential influence during its assessment period. That finding is an evaluation of political leverage, rather than a claim that ministries have no responsibilities. Assessing an executive initiative consequently requires examining both its formal mandate and evidence of its implementation. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
